module sh.sysl.ogol

// What an Ogol expression can produce, and how it is written back.
//
// **Three kinds and a fourth that is an absence.** Logo has one datum -- the word -- and gets numbers
// and booleans by reading a word when it needs one, so `"5` and `5` are the same thing there and
// `"true` is what `if` tests. Ogol separates them, because the reason it exists is to write to a
// register: a value that knows whether it is a number decides at the point of the mistake rather than
// at the point of the write, and `12` meaning twelve everywhere is worth more here than a uniform
// datum is.
//
// `Nothing` is what a command answers with. Logo draws the same line and calls the two sides commands
// and operations; keeping the absence *in* the value type rather than beside it is what lets one
// evaluator return both, and what lets `print print 1` be refused with a sentence about `print`
// rather than about a missing argument.
enum Value
    Nothing
    Num(n: long)
    Word(s: string)
    Bool(b: bool)
end Value

// A value as a person would type it back in. It is the whole of Ogol's output -- `print` renders, the
// REPL renders what a line came to, and a diagnostic renders whatever it is complaining about -- so a
// value that rendered ambiguously would be a hole in all three at once.
render(v: Value) -> string
    v match
        Nothing -> ""
        Num(n) -> str(n)
        Word(s) -> s
        Bool(b) -> if b then "true" else "false"
end render

// The name of a value's kind, for a diagnostic that has to say what it got. Written as the noun a
// sentence wants -- "a number", not "Num" -- because every use of it is inside prose.
kind(v: Value) -> string
    v match
        Nothing -> "nothing"
        Num(_) -> "a number"
        Word(_) -> "a word"
        Bool(_) -> "a boolean"
end kind

// Equality over the whole of `Value`, which the language needs for `=` and `<>`.
//
// **Values of different kinds are unequal rather than an error.** `1 = "1` is a fair question with a
// plain answer, and refusing it would mean a program could not compare two things it had not already
// proved were the same kind. Ordering is the other way about -- see `sh.sysl.ogol.compare` -- because
// there is no answer to whether a word is less than a boolean.
same(a: Value, b: Value) -> bool
    a match
        Nothing -> b match
            Nothing -> true
            _ -> false
        Num(x) -> b match
            Num(y) -> x == y
            _ -> false
        Word(x) -> b match
            Word(y) -> x == y
            _ -> false
        Bool(x) -> b match
            Bool(y) -> x == y
            _ -> false
end same
